Ultrahigh-energy cosmic rays (UHECRs) are charged nuclei with energies above roughly 10^18 eV. As they propagate through intergalactic space they interact with extragalactic background photon fields (the cosmic microwave background and the extragalactic background light), producing neutral secondary messengers — cosmogenic neutrinos and cosmogenic gamma rays — via processes such as photo-pion production on protons, photo-disintegration of heavier nuclei, and subsequent decays of the resulting unstable particles. The expected diffuse fluxes of these cosmogenic neutrinos and gamma rays at Earth depend on a multi-dimensional space of astrophysical and particle-physics parameters, including:

(a) the chemical composition of UHECRs injected at their sources (the mix of nuclear species and the spectral indices of each component);
(b) the maximum rigidity or maximum energy attainable at the sources;
(c) the cosmological source-evolution model, i.e. the comoving source density as a function of redshift z;
(d) the structure and strength of intergalactic and Galactic magnetic fields, which affect propagation horizons and arrival directions;
(e) the choice of hadronic interaction model used to describe air-shower observables and to constrain (a)–(c) from UHECR data.

The problem is the following:

(i) Determine, as a function of energy E_ν (for neutrinos) and E_γ (for gamma rays), the all-flavor cosmogenic neutrino flux Φ_ν(E_ν) and the diffuse cosmogenic gamma-ray flux Φ_γ(E_γ) expected at Earth, scanning over the entire region of parameter space (a)–(e) that is currently allowed by measurements of the UHECR energy spectrum and mass composition. Provide the full envelope of predictions together with a quantitative characterization of the uncertainties induced by each ingredient, in particular the magnetic-field configurations and the hadronic-interaction models.

(ii) Identify which observable features of Φ_ν(E_ν) and Φ_γ(E_γ) (e.g. peak energy, normalization, spectral shape, flavor ratios, anisotropies, correlations between the two channels) are diagnostic of the underlying UHECR source population, and quantify what can be inferred from a measurement of these fluxes about the sources of UHECRs — including their chemical composition, maximum energy, and cosmological evolution.

